+    # set entirely, which is the actual strict point for this method.
+    _SPEC_CASC_TOK_ALPHA = float("-inf")
+    _SPEC_CASC_TOK_ALPHA_SOURCE = f"default, no readable {_SPEC_CASC_TOK_ALPHA_FILE}"
+
+# SEMANTIC GUARD, AND-VARIANT (analysis/semantic_guard/, not upstream vLLM,
+# not part of Xia et al.'s taxonomy). Sibling of spec_casc_tok_semantic_guard
+# with a different combination rule, built after finding that patch's own
+# loophole: spec-casc-tok's own pi_rej formula is NOT uniformly more lenient
+# than strict -- for a drafted token OUTSIDE the trusted top set A, pi_rej(v)
+# = eta*p(v) < p(v) (since eta = 1 - sum_A q(u) < 1), so its effective accept
+# ratio eta*(p/q) is strictly BELOW the lossless ratio p/q. spec-casc-tok is
+# therefore *more conservative* than lossless for exactly these tokens --
+# empirically confirmed to be disproportionately common at hesitation-marker
+# positions (6.6% of guarded positions vs 2.2% background rate, see
+# analysis/semantic_guard/README.md). The original semantic-guard patch
+# OVERRIDES to pure strict at guarded positions, which means it sometimes
+# ACCEPTS a hesitation-marker token that plain spec_casc_tok's own relaxed
+# test would have rejected -- i.e. that guard can make hesitation MORE
+# frequent than doing nothing, the opposite of its intent (confirmed: 319 of
+# 4,830 guarded AIME24 positions are exactly this case, and are providably
+# 100% outside the trusted set, since in-set tokens are ALWAYS accepted by
+# spec-casc-tok's own rule and could never produce this direction of
+# disagreement).
